{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"refreshing-cosmic-jaeger","creation_channel":"mcp","description":"## Symptom\n\n`review_plan_start` returned a **stale BLOCK** for a plan whose material had just been widened, instead of reviewing the new material. The returned `durable` block was the attestation from the *previous* run.\n\nObserved on story `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`:\n\n1. `set_file_impact` → 13 paths.\n2. `review_plan_start` → job `…dbe7ada2`, `verdict: PASS`, `signed_at: 1793154820371400435`.\n3. `set_file_impact` → **14** paths (added `tests/unit/workflow/test_contracts.py`).\n4. `review_plan_start` → job `…41c67599`, **`verdict: BLOCK`**, `durable.verdict: \"stale-material\"`, `reason: \"the plan was materially edited since review — changed: file_impact (13 -> 14 paths)\"`, and `signed_at: 1793154820371400435` — **the timestamp from step 2**.\n5. `show_ticket` reported `plan_reviews: 0` — no findings were produced for the step-4 job.\n6. `review_plan_start(force=True)` → job `…702b44ed`, `verdict: PASS`, `durable.verdict: certified`, `signed_at: 1793158405677634370`. Same tree, same SHA `ee5253abfd4`.\n\nThe only difference between steps 4 and 6 is `force=True`.\n\n## Diagnosis\n\nThe start path's de-dup key does not include the plan material fingerprint. Step 4 matched the step-2 job on ticket + basis, attached to it, and surfaced its already-computed result — which, re-evaluated against the now-changed material, renders as `stale-material`. No fresh review ran, which is why `plan_reviews` stayed empty.\n\nThe tool's own contract says \"Duplicate ticket/basis starts share a job\". The defect is that **a material edit does not make a start non-duplicate**, even though a material edit is precisely what invalidates the prior result.\n\n## Why this is worth fixing rather than documenting\n\nThe failure is silent and inverted. An agent that widens `file_impact` — which AGENTS.md actively requires when a change touches a file the plan did not declare — and then re-reviews gets a BLOCK verdict carrying a plausible, *accurate-sounding* reason (\"the plan was materially edited since review\"). The natural reading is \"my plan is blocked, go revise it\". The correct reading is \"your start was swallowed; re-run with force\". Nothing in the response distinguishes those.\n\nWorse, `gate_status` reported `status: \"passed\"` and `findings.readable: true` with a `current-review-result-sidecar` for the step-4 job, so the surfaces that an agent would use to sanity-check disagree with each other.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] A `review_plan_start` issued after the ticket's reviewed material changed (description or `file_impact`) does **not** de-dup onto a job started before that change; it runs a fresh review.\n- [ ] Regression test: start a review, mutate `file_impact`, start again without `force`, and assert the second job produces its own findings and a certified attestation rather than replaying the first job's verdict.\n- [ ] `verify_completion_start` is audited for the same de-dup key defect and fixed or shown to be unaffected.\n- [ ] If de-dup across a material change is ever intentional, the response must say so explicitly rather than surfacing a bare `stale-material` that reads as a plan defect.\n\nDiscovered while implementing story `6b66-9378-ebb1-446c`; cost one confused round trip.","id":"dc9b-e792-bad3-4578","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":["mcp","plan-review-gate","correctness"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"review_plan_start de-dups across a material change and replays the prior run's stale verdict"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793158599954089388,"uuid":"89edb252-705f-4cbd-b0b5-f051fb9b37e8"}